malala yosef sai it is time to take  action outspoken critic of the taliban  the youngest nobel peace prize laureate  tireless campaigner for girls education  mark international women's day malala  met with the economist's editor-in-chief  zanny minton bedos to discuss the  effects of conflict on girls education  [Music]  right now i'm sure you along with all of  us are watching with horror the the  scenes in ukraine a country that you  know  only a couple of weeks ago was was a  peaceful european country and suddenly  there are extraordinary pictures of kids  as refugees kids in bomb shelters what  goes through your mind when you see that  it is heartbreaking to see  these images of  people collecting their belongings and  leaving their homes uh and i always tell  people that  becoming a refugee is no one's first  choice it's always the last and it's  always the only option that they have  left and we see that in ukraine right  now and  and and we all know that wars cause  uh  irreversible and unimaginable damage but  i think it's really important for all of  us to realize that we need to come  together and ask for an end to these  wars whether that is in yemen whether  that is in palestine whether that is in  kashmir or  afghanistan or in ukraine or any other  country right now is there a message  that you would have for the young people  of ukraine right now  i want to tell  everyone in ukraine especially  young girls that  you know we stand with you and none of  this is your fault and this should not  be happening and i hope that this is  also a time of  awakening for all of us about  how these conflicts affect so many  countries like when you look at  afghanistan or other countries you know  these countries were not always poor and  these countries were not always in these  crisis something happened abruptly and  things changed the same was the story of  swat valley as well it was a place of  tourism and the queen was visiting and  we you know we had a good economy and  then all of a sudden people appear with  guns and bombs and and you are like  what's happening in these words like  things get so complicated that  uh you know that in the end you just  sort of forget to ask this basic  question which is why is this happening  why can't we put an end to these  conflicts uh so i hope that you know  that it wakes us up is there anything  that we've learned from the pandemic and  the ability to do remote learning the  improvements in technology that can be  helpful for children of conflict in  future i think uh there are  opportunities but there are challenges  as well in digital education i think we  need to take full advantage of it but  also  ensure that we are  addressing the gender gap in access to  digital tools some of the research work  that we have done in nigeria and  in pakistan showed us that girls are  less likely to have access to digital  tools  in their homes especially when there's  one digital device it's more likely that  the boy has access to it or even if she  has access to it she's more busy in the  house chores and she does not get enough  time to be to be learning and studying  when leaders make policies for digital  education they have to ensure that  they make gender-inclusive policies for  the digital education future that we see  can we turn out to afghanistan which is  another i mean almost unfathomable  humanitarian tragedy and at the same  time  a government that clearly does not share  your views on  girls education  how does the malala fund operate there  at this point so malala fund  was supporting activists in afghanistan  and in this critical times you have to  ensure that they are safe  and because of that association with  malala fund a lot of them uh were in a  very difficult situation so  most of them are now based in different  countries or have uh found refuge in in  in their final destination now uh and  all of them are still engaging in  advocacy for girls education for women's  rights and protection right now and we  support them  regarding afghanistan this is a time  where  one leaders can show whether they are  truly committed to girls education or  not afghanistan right now is the only  country where girls are facing  difficulties in their secondary  education they  they are basically facing a de facto ban  on their secondary education  and the taliban will continue to make  excuses from lack of female teachers or  safety issues on their way to schools to  prevent girls from getting back into  schools so we need to ensure that those  children can enroll back into schools  and that the girls who will be returning  back to school that they receive quality  education what is deeply concerning is  that the curriculum plans that we see  already from the taliban they are taking  out some of the key subjects and we need  to ensure that you know children are  learning about sciences and social  studies so we need to ensure that the  education that they get is quality  education how do you think western  policymakers can really influence that i  mean what are their tools that you think  are not being used approaches that are  not being used because at the moment  everyone  seems almost helpless at watching this  i think you know right now there are  just so many things that need to happen  because we know that  afghanistan  is is is literally  you know on the verge of a collapse an  economic collapse right now  and i hope that first of all these  international communities  in all of the conversations and  dialogues that they're having with the  taliban that they make no compromise on  the protection of  women's rights girls education and  minorities rights that has to be their  top priority and i think along with that  there is need for financing  there's need for humanitarian fund uh  but  there's also the issue of uh of you know  this this traditional definition of  humanitarian fund that does not include  education so there's another push to uh  encourage these leaders to help in  education as well to ensure that we are  actually helping the afghanistan  communities and and people to  actually be prepared for their future  and to rebuild their economy and i hope  that president biden and the u.s courts  they  make  um  they make just decisions for the  afghan reserves you know that money  belongs to the afghan people and it  should go back to them it should be  helping them in recovering from this  crisis like we know that millions of  afghan people  are  at risk of starvation  we know that many of them are you know  are right now selling their daughters to  help their families to  survive  so when you see these kind of news it  breaks your heart uh because you know  that this is not the life that they  should be facing right now and this is  something that  they that  the western countries or other  neighboring countries that could prevent  and i would also add that there is a  strong role for the muslim countries to  play in this time right now  the rule is critical i hope that oic and  other muslim countries together stand up  for the protection of women and girls  because the taliban basically misuse the  name of islam so other muslim countries  need to stand up and say that  in their countries which are a dozen  there are dozens of muslim countries  that in their countries  women have access to education they do  jobs they go to work they don't need a  male companion to go from one city to  another uh they can earn f for  themselves uh and and i think you know  they need to define  what it means in islam to have equality  for women to have education for for  girls so  they need to challenge the taliban  quality education  is urgent it is important  it is important for peace and stability  it is important for democracy it is  important for making informed decisions  uh and it is  important for our own empowerment and  self-care as well thanks for watching if  you'd like to find out more about our  fort